We'll also cover Eric Adams and how they're trying&nbsp; to get him out of the race by bribing him. It's&nbsp;&nbsp; probably not a feeling he's unfamiliar with.&nbsp; We'll also talk about sanctions on Palestinians,&nbsp;&nbsp; nothing new there, but this time it's for having&nbsp; the temerity to oppose genocide. We'll get into&nbsp;&nbsp; the details. And this part, I don't know if&nbsp; you care about, John, but you fly a lot. Trump&nbsp;&nbsp; has decided to screw airline passengers, and I&nbsp; wonder if he'll pay a price for that in terms&nbsp;&nbsp; of populism, siding with one of the most hated&nbsp; industries in America, the airlines, against&nbsp;&nbsp; ordinary passengers. So, let's just get to it.&nbsp; What do you want to talk about first, John?&nbsp; I'd like to talk about Eric&nbsp; Adams first, if we could.&nbsp; Absolutely. Deep down, I still have a love for&nbsp; how the sausage is made at the State Department&nbsp;&nbsp; and in foreign policy. One thing about the Saudis,&nbsp; and this is why Saudi Arabia is being floated as a&nbsp;&nbsp; place for Eric Adams to end up, is that they have&nbsp; always insisted on not having a career diplomat&nbsp;&nbsp; as the U.S. ambassador. They demand a political&nbsp; appointee, which is exactly the opposite of most&nbsp;&nbsp; other countries. Why? Because they want to be&nbsp; able to pick up the phone, call the ambassador,&nbsp;&nbsp; and have the ambassador call the president, and&nbsp; the president takes the call. If you're a career&nbsp;&nbsp; diplomat, the president has never heard of you.&nbsp; You can't call the White House in the middle of&nbsp;&nbsp; the night and say, "The King of Saudi Arabia wants&nbsp; to talk to you." As crazy as this might sound,&nbsp;&nbsp; the Saudis would be very happy to have Eric&nbsp; Adams as the U.S. ambassador. That's not to&nbsp;&nbsp; say that 51 U.S. senators will agree to have&nbsp; Eric Adams as the U.S. ambassador. Although,&nbsp;&nbsp; if he were to drop out of the race and the&nbsp; Republicans endorse Cuomo, he'll still lose.&nbsp;&nbsp; We need to break this down for people who are not&nbsp; watching the details of the New York City mayoral&nbsp;&nbsp; race, which is super important, even if you don't&nbsp; live in or care about New York, just because it's&nbsp;&nbsp; the epic showdown inside the Democratic Party&nbsp; between the progressive left base of the party&nbsp;&nbsp; and the corporatist centrists who run the DNC. In the primary, Mamdani, the democratic socialist,&nbsp;&nbsp; basically a Bernie acolyte, managed to defeat a&nbsp; crowded field and is now the Democratic nominee in&nbsp;&nbsp; the general election. Current polls, I just&nbsp; checked the RealClearPolitics averages,&nbsp;&nbsp; show Zohran Mamdani at 38.2%. In a distant second&nbsp; is Andrew Cuomo, the former New York governor,&nbsp;&nbsp; running as a third-party candidate on the Fight&nbsp; Anti-Semitism line. Subtle. Cygni Wilkinson,&nbsp;&nbsp; the editorial cartoonist for the Philadelphia&nbsp; Inquirer, is delightful, and she says in moments&nbsp;&nbsp; like that, which you just reminded me of. Curtis&nbsp; Sliwa, the founder of the Guardian Angels and the&nbsp;&nbsp; perennial Republican nominee, is at 16.6%.&nbsp; Basically, as popular as lint is the sitting&nbsp;&nbsp; incumbent mayor, Eric Adams, who is currently at&nbsp; 10.6%. So, the powers that be are looking at one&nbsp;&nbsp; outlying poll that seems to indicate that if you&nbsp; could get Adams and hopefully Sliwa to drop out,&nbsp;&nbsp; but certainly Adams, you might actually be able&nbsp; to beat Mamdani with Cuomo. The billionaire class,&nbsp;&nbsp; real estate interests, Wall Street, all those&nbsp; people—that's their plot here, right? They're&nbsp;&nbsp; trying to clear the field for Cuomo. Oddly,&nbsp; the New York Post, the Murdoch organ here&nbsp;&nbsp; in New York, is still trying to push for Adams. Honestly, Clovis, my cat, has a far better chance&nbsp;&nbsp; of being mayor than Adams. Seriously, there's&nbsp; no way. He's at 10.6% in the race, and he's the&nbsp;&nbsp; incumbent. Mamdani is increasing in the polls.&nbsp; Adams has faced many very credible corruption&nbsp;&nbsp; charges. I can't say that he's corrupt, but in my&nbsp; opinion, he is corrupt and takes bribes. I think&nbsp;&nbsp; that's the opinion of most New Yorkers, too. So,&nbsp; the idea is to get rid of Adams. If Adams drops&nbsp;&nbsp; out and endorses Cuomo, they'll work on Sliwa.&nbsp; Sliwa says there's absolutely nothing that can&nbsp;&nbsp; convince him to drop out. He said he'd have to&nbsp; be run over by a car to drop out of the race.&nbsp; I kind of believe Curtis. It just seems very&nbsp; desperate, right? Steve Witkoff, the Trump envoy&nbsp;&nbsp; to various global hotspots, including Russia&nbsp; and Ukraine, is floating this. We don't know&nbsp;&nbsp; how far up the food chain this has gone. We don't&nbsp; know if the Donald is on board with this, or if&nbsp;&nbsp; he's just reading about it. How likely is this? You know what? I think it's actually likely&nbsp;&nbsp; because Donald Trump isn't stupid. We like to&nbsp; criticize Donald Trump, sure, but he's not stupid,&nbsp;&nbsp; and his political instincts are obvious. If he&nbsp; were a stupid man, he'd be in jail right now. He&nbsp;&nbsp; wouldn't be the first person elected president&nbsp; without military or political experience.&nbsp;&nbsp; He's not stupid. He knows that Eric Adams isn't&nbsp; going to win this race. There's a better chance&nbsp;&nbsp; that Trump can have some serious input if he&nbsp; gets Adams out. So, what do you do? You offer&nbsp;&nbsp; him the ambassadorship to Saudi Arabia. Why not?&nbsp; That will make him happy, and the Saudis want&nbsp;&nbsp; a political appointee anyway. Then, he at least&nbsp; throws a little wrench into the Mamdani campaign,&nbsp;&nbsp; even if Trump has already concluded that Mamdani&nbsp; is going to win. Getting Eric Adams out of the&nbsp;&nbsp; race at least forces Mamdani to spend some money&nbsp; and allows people to coalesce around the candidate&nbsp;&nbsp; Trump wants, which is neither Curtis Sliwa nor&nbsp; Andrew Cuomo. It just makes it a little tougher&nbsp;&nbsp; for Mamdani. Mamdani has already got the maximum&nbsp; number of campaign contributions he's allowed&nbsp;&nbsp; to accept. People are throwing so much money at&nbsp; him. It's hard to see how he loses at this stage,&nbsp;&nbsp; and he's increasing in the polls. The more people&nbsp; pay attention to him, the more they like him in&nbsp;&nbsp; New York. I think all this establishment stuff is&nbsp; just counterproductive. It's just blowback, right?&nbsp; It's super interesting to see how this is all&nbsp; playing out. I guess Eric Adams gets a soft&nbsp;&nbsp; landing. How is that post, though? We talked&nbsp; yesterday about the ambassadorial position&nbsp;&nbsp; in Athens, which all humans can agree is a nice&nbsp; job. How is Riyadh? You and I have similar views&nbsp;&nbsp; on what it's like to live in Saudi Arabia. My personal view is that Saudi Arabia is the&nbsp;&nbsp; biggest open-air prison in the world, but that's&nbsp; just me. When I was serving in Saudi Arabia,&nbsp;&nbsp; I served under two ambassadors. One was Chas&nbsp; Freeman, the legendary Chas Freeman, who,&nbsp;&nbsp; God bless him, is still alive. He has some health&nbsp; problems, but he is one of the leading voices out&nbsp;&nbsp; there in support of the Palestinian people. He&nbsp; went on to be the Assistant Secretary of Defense&nbsp;&nbsp; and was supposed to be chairman of the National&nbsp; Intelligence Council, but the Obama people killed&nbsp;&nbsp; the nomination because he's pro-Palestinian. The&nbsp; second ambassador I served under had been the&nbsp;&nbsp; governor of Mississippi and then Bill Clinton's&nbsp; Secretary of the Navy. Nice guy, sweet guy,&nbsp;&nbsp; completely out of his element. He just did what he&nbsp; was told to do. Whoever was the National Security&nbsp;&nbsp; Advisor, Sandy Berger for most of that period,&nbsp; just told him what to do, and he did it. That's&nbsp;&nbsp; what the Saudis wanted. If you're the ambassador,&nbsp; life is good because you're meeting with the king,&nbsp;&nbsp; the crown prince, and the minister of foreign&nbsp; affairs. You have the biggest parties in the&nbsp;&nbsp; country and the biggest mansion in the diplomatic&nbsp; quarter. Life's going to be good. As soon as you&nbsp;&nbsp; step out of that house, life’s not so good because&nbsp; Saudi Arabia is a terrible, terrible place to be.&nbsp;&nbsp; I’d rather be almost anywhere else than Saudi&nbsp; Arabia. The ambassador lives in a compound,&nbsp;&nbsp; can have alcohol, has high walls, and can&nbsp; pretty much do whatever they want. They’re&nbsp;&nbsp; living in their own private Idaho, right?&nbsp; Like a wild potato. Don’t go on the patio.&nbsp; But that’s basically it. You’re in your Wally&nbsp; World. It’s awful. Every once in a while, you get&nbsp;&nbsp; in the car and go to Dhahran to meet the people at&nbsp; the consulate there. Then you take a quick flight&nbsp;&nbsp; to Jeddah. The Saudi government moves to Jeddah in&nbsp; May, June, July, and August because Riyadh is too&nbsp;&nbsp; hot—120, 125 degrees. They go to Jeddah because&nbsp; it’s on the water, and you get a little breeze.&nbsp;&nbsp; It’s still 120 degrees, but it’s all relative,&nbsp; I suppose. The reason they want you in the first&nbsp;&nbsp; place is that they want someone whose phone calls&nbsp; the president will take. That’s the bottom line.&nbsp; Adam wants to know, is it true that if you want to&nbsp; work in Saudi Arabia, you’re appointed a handler&nbsp;&nbsp; by the government, your passport is taken away,&nbsp; and you can only leave with their permission? Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp; that is true. It’s a serious human rights&nbsp; problem. You have to be sponsored. On your visa&nbsp;&nbsp; application, you must state who your sponsor is. A&nbsp; Saudi must be the majority shareholder of whatever&nbsp;&nbsp; company you’re working for. You can’t just go to&nbsp; Saudi Arabia and say, “I’m going to open an LLC&nbsp;&nbsp; and make widgets.” No, you own 49%, and some&nbsp; random Saudi owns 51%. The Saudi holds your&nbsp;&nbsp; passport, and if they don’t like you or you have&nbsp; a falling out over money, you’re essentially under&nbsp;&nbsp; house arrest because you can’t go anywhere. That’s&nbsp; how they keep, especially South Asian women, as&nbsp;&nbsp; sex slaves. They hire them as domestics, nannies,&nbsp; or English teachers, and when they arrive, the&nbsp;&nbsp; sponsor takes their passport and says, “Now take&nbsp; your clothes off, and you’re going to live in the&nbsp;&nbsp; cupboard under the stairs.” Lovely. Best system. I&nbsp; hated it there. Good thing we’re so close to them.&nbsp; Marble wants to know, and thanks for the generous&nbsp; donation—thanks also to Houdini and everyone&nbsp;&nbsp; else—your thoughts on Nigel Farage. As recently&nbsp; as five years ago, Nigel Farage was a joke, a kind&nbsp;&nbsp; of neo-Nazi fascist weirdo from the British right&nbsp; wing. Over the last five years, with the electoral&nbsp;&nbsp; humiliation that the Conservative Party underwent,&nbsp; Farage has emerged as a real voice on the right.&nbsp;&nbsp; He’s no less fascistic than he was five years&nbsp; ago, but he’s been accepted by the mainstream&nbsp;&nbsp; as a legitimate right-wing voice. When I was last&nbsp; in London in March, there was an article in The&nbsp;&nbsp; Times of London, a conservative paper owned by&nbsp; Rupert Murdoch, saying that Nigel Farage would&nbsp;&nbsp; likely be the next leader of the Conservative&nbsp; Party. If that happens, I could see the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp; Conservative Party as a viable political force.&nbsp; Conservatives who aren’t that conservative, called&nbsp;&nbsp; “wets,” would move to the Liberal Party, which&nbsp; is not liberal but actually quite conservative,&nbsp;&nbsp; especially on foreign policy. The Nigel Farage&nbsp; conservatives would just be a rump party.&nbsp; Farage is a very interesting character because&nbsp; he reminds me of what I’ve read happened in the&nbsp;&nbsp; UK in conservative politics in the run-up to the&nbsp; Second World War. There were a lot of Brits who&nbsp;&nbsp; thought Hitler was A-OK, and it took years for&nbsp; them to choose sides, like the Oswald Mosley&nbsp;&nbsp; types. The British Fascist Party&nbsp; was a real thing, and there were&nbsp;&nbsp; members of the royal family who were part of it. Thank you so much to Peter for becoming a monthly&nbsp;&nbsp; supporter. Let’s talk about the politics here.&nbsp; I’ve got to think that the reason Trump’s not all&nbsp;&nbsp; in on this plan is that he knows it probably won’t&nbsp; work. He doesn’t want to get behind a plan that’s&nbsp;&nbsp; likely to fail, and then he’ll be made fun of,&nbsp; which he hates. There are a lot of moving parts&nbsp;&nbsp; here. In the end, Donald Trump does things that&nbsp; benefit Donald Trump. How does this benefit him?&nbsp;&nbsp; It doesn’t really. I think the Republican&nbsp; Party is planning to go after Mamdani and&nbsp;&nbsp; turn him into their punching bag for the next&nbsp; four years. I wouldn’t be so sure it’s going&nbsp;&nbsp; to work. He seems like a really smart young man&nbsp; who’s surrounding himself with older, seasoned&nbsp;&nbsp; New Yorkers who will guide him at Gracie Mansion&nbsp; and City Hall. They’ll tell him how things work.&nbsp; I don’t think he’s going to go in there like a&nbsp; bull in a china shop. He’s been running a great&nbsp;&nbsp; campaign. When Mamdani came out ahead, many in&nbsp; the center and on the right said it was a fluke,&nbsp;&nbsp; temporary, and that everyone would coalesce around&nbsp; Cuomo, and the DNC would live happily ever after.&nbsp;&nbsp; That’s not happening. His political godfather&nbsp; happens to be Jewish, so when people say,&nbsp;&nbsp; “Mamdani’s going to open ovens for the&nbsp; Jews,” no, he’s not. New York voters,&nbsp;&nbsp; many of whom are Jewish, are voting&nbsp; for him, and they know what he’s about.&nbsp; Nixon Barley says all of Trump’s advisors are&nbsp; young guys. I’ve got to tell you, though, he&nbsp;&nbsp; had that guy—his name escapes me—who ran against&nbsp; him in the primary and then endorsed him. They&nbsp;&nbsp; kind of co-endorsed each other in the primary,&nbsp; and he’s older than we are. He’s holding strong.&nbsp; Before we move on, we’ve got a good question&nbsp; from Road Beef. If we mentioned Farage,&nbsp;&nbsp; I must also ask Ted and John for their opinion&nbsp; on Boris Johnson—two peas in a pod. Nobody in&nbsp;&nbsp; the UK was more shocked when Boris Johnson&nbsp; became prime minister than Boris Johnson&nbsp;&nbsp; himself. He was a provocateur journalist&nbsp; who made a career by pissing people off on&nbsp;&nbsp; purpose. He was one of those original “I&nbsp; have an opinion” right-wing journalists,&nbsp;&nbsp; the “what are you going to do about it?” kind of&nbsp; people. He got into politics just to troll people,&nbsp;&nbsp; and one crazy thing led to another, and he found&nbsp; himself prime minister. Every time I go to the UK,&nbsp;&nbsp; I visit my host family, who sponsored me when&nbsp; I was 19. They’re absolutely wonderful people.&nbsp;&nbsp; My host father was a member of parliament&nbsp; in the 1980s for the Liberal Party.&nbsp;&nbsp; He’s in his late eighties now but&nbsp; still 100% sharp and follows every&nbsp;&nbsp; detail of politics. He said at the time that&nbsp; Johnson never wanted to be prime minister. It&nbsp;&nbsp; was all about lighting a fire under people and&nbsp; then writing a book about it later to promote&nbsp;&nbsp; himself on TV. He didn’t think he’d actually&nbsp; become prime minister. It was like Trump—there&nbsp;&nbsp; are a lot of dogs catching cars nowadays. For&nbsp; Trump, it was about enhancing the brand. He&nbsp;&nbsp; didn’t think he was going to win. So, you find&nbsp; yourself prime minister of the UK, yelling about&nbsp;&nbsp; Brexit for months, and you’ve got to Brexit. Two great lines from the scroll. Houdini says,&nbsp;&nbsp; “Mamdani, regardless of what he says during&nbsp; the campaign, if he wins, he’ll get the talk&nbsp;&nbsp; and fall in line, just like AOC did.” James&nbsp; Gernales says, “Youth and cunning will always&nbsp;&nbsp; be outdone by age and treachery.” Amen. That’s a&nbsp; tattoo-worthy line. It’s an ugly world we live in.&nbsp; Ross Houston wants to know how valuable access to&nbsp; a high-level member of the UAE’s economy cabinet&nbsp;&nbsp; would be to the CIA. Not very valuable. The CIA&nbsp; doesn’t really spy on the UAE because they give&nbsp;&nbsp; us everything we want. We don’t care about&nbsp; their economy. What we care about most is&nbsp;&nbsp; their commitment to the alliance with the United&nbsp; States against Iran and the Houthis. The Brits&nbsp;&nbsp; make the UAE economy run—they’re the bankers&nbsp; and advisors managing the National Wealth Fund.&nbsp; Speaking of the talk, if young bloods like Mamdani&nbsp; get the talk from old-school dudes who tell them&nbsp;&nbsp; what’s what, I think Trump has a boss too—the&nbsp; corporations. Trump obviously got a call from&nbsp;&nbsp; the airline CEOs, saying, “Don’t you dare go ahead&nbsp; with this last-second Biden-Buttigieg plan that&nbsp;&nbsp; would have required airlines to pay passengers&nbsp; when delays are their fault, not due to weather.”&nbsp;&nbsp; Remember when Southwest failed to upgrade their&nbsp; computer systems, and people got delayed? If&nbsp;&nbsp; you fly, you know how aggravating this is. It&nbsp; happened to my son recently. He’s changing planes,&nbsp;&nbsp; the airline messed up, he misses his connection,&nbsp; and he’s stuck in a strange city. They’re like,&nbsp;&nbsp; “You can sleep in the airport overnight, or you&nbsp; can shell out for a hotel on your own dime and&nbsp;&nbsp; pay for extra meals.” In Europe, they have a&nbsp; system—imperfect, but if the airline messes up,&nbsp;&nbsp; you can put in a claim, and they have to&nbsp; compensate you. Buttigieg and outgoing Biden&nbsp;&nbsp; pushed this through, like too many good things&nbsp; they came up with, in a cowardly way on their&nbsp;&nbsp; way out the door. It never went into effect,&nbsp; and now Trump is quietly getting rid of it on&nbsp;&nbsp; a Friday. Airlines are scum. They make billions&nbsp; in profits, squeezing us in. It’s unsafe to fly.&nbsp;&nbsp; I’m 6’2” and jammed in the seats, but that’s&nbsp; not my main concern. Deep vein thrombosis I&nbsp;&nbsp; can live and die with, but if you ever had&nbsp; to evacuate a plane, which I’ve had to do,&nbsp;&nbsp; you can’t because the aisles are too narrow.&nbsp; It’s a safety issue. This is such a corporatist,&nbsp;&nbsp; Reagan-type move. By itself, it’s not enough to&nbsp; destroy Trump’s coalition, but if there were more&nbsp;&nbsp; of this, do you think Trump would lose populist&nbsp; conservatives like producer Robbie, who voted for&nbsp;&nbsp; him, or would they just not be aware of it? Unfortunately, I think it’s the latter. Most&nbsp;&nbsp; people aren’t paying attention. Americans have&nbsp; accepted being treated like cattle. I was in&nbsp;&nbsp; Spain earlier this year and had to fly&nbsp; from Madrid to Dublin, then Dublin to&nbsp;&nbsp; Washington. There was a mechanical problem in&nbsp; Madrid. They fixed the plane, but I missed my&nbsp;&nbsp; connection in Dublin. Since it wasn’t my fault, I&nbsp; went to the information desk and said I was on the&nbsp;&nbsp; flight from Madrid. They said, “We’ve been waiting&nbsp; for you, Mr. Kiriakou. Unfortunately, there’s no&nbsp;&nbsp; flight to Washington until tomorrow. We’ve booked&nbsp; you in a four-star hotel with a buffet breakfast&nbsp;&nbsp; that could feed an army, and we’re refunding&nbsp; $600 of your $1,200 ticket.” I went to the hotel,&nbsp;&nbsp; they had a shuttle bus, expedited check-in, a&nbsp; gorgeous hotel in the center of Dublin, a gigantic&nbsp;&nbsp; buffet. I did some sightseeing, flew to Washington&nbsp; the next morning, and six weeks later, I got $600&nbsp;&nbsp; transferred into my checking account. Why don’t&nbsp; we do that? We should. Even with weather delays,&nbsp;&nbsp; there should be some compensation. Maybe&nbsp; airlines could charge an extra fee for a&nbsp;&nbsp; compensation fund. It’s not their fault if there’s&nbsp; a storm, but people need to stay at a hotel.&nbsp; DeWolfson, thank you so much for becoming a&nbsp; monthly supporter. This airline news stuck in&nbsp;&nbsp; my craw, and I noticed it was a Friday news&nbsp; dump when people are outside enjoying the&nbsp;&nbsp; weekend. They know this isn’t popular. I still&nbsp; maintain Pete Buttigieg is the front-runner for&nbsp;&nbsp; the Democratic nomination in 2028, not Gavin&nbsp; Newsom. If I’m Buttigieg, I’m doing a little&nbsp;&nbsp; dance because I can say, “I promoted this,&nbsp; and Trump killed it. I’m on your side. Trump&nbsp;&nbsp; is on the airlines’ and corporations’ side.” Here’s an ad: Thanks, everyone, for listening.&nbsp;&nbsp; If you haven’t already, make sure you download&nbsp; the Rumble app. It’s free, simple to use,&nbsp;&nbsp; and the best way to stay connected to the content&nbsp; you want. Search for our channel and hit follow so&nbsp;&nbsp; you’ll get a notification every time we go live.&nbsp; No missed shows, no extra steps. Grab it using&nbsp;&nbsp; the link in the description or the pinned live&nbsp; chat, download it to your phone, and you’re ready&nbsp;&nbsp; to go. If you prefer watching on the big screen,&nbsp; Rumble is available on all major streaming devices&nbsp;&nbsp; and smart TVs. Just search for Rumble, follow&nbsp; the channel, and be part of the action. Please&nbsp;&nbsp; watch us on Rumble because that’s where we make&nbsp; the financial action. Thanks to our viewers and&nbsp;&nbsp; producer Robbie, we were on Rumble’s front page&nbsp; the other day, boosting our viewership by about&nbsp;&nbsp; 9 or 10%. It’s insanely appreciated. Peter says&nbsp; every airport should have Dante’s Divine Comedy&nbsp;&nbsp; inscribed: “Abandon all hope, ye who enter here.”&nbsp; It’s a bleak feeling walking into an airport. Even&nbsp;&nbsp; with TSA PreCheck, it’s an undignified experience.&nbsp; They’re like, “We found your applesauce,&nbsp;&nbsp; criminal.” I’d get rid of TSA entirely and go back&nbsp; to the old days when you could walk your loved&nbsp;&nbsp; ones to the gate. My son thought I was kidding&nbsp; when I told him that. When I was a boy visiting&nbsp;&nbsp; relatives in Washington, D.C., my dad took me to&nbsp; National Airport. There was an observation deck on&nbsp;&nbsp; the roof of the terminal where you could watch&nbsp; planes take off and land. It was awesome for a&nbsp;&nbsp; kid. Now kids are like, “That never happened.” In the early ’90s, my sister-in-law couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp; use the return on her round-trip ticket. I&nbsp; used it, and they didn’t even check my ID.&nbsp;&nbsp; Her name was Alice, and the guy gave me a funny&nbsp; look. I said, “Like Alice Cooper,” and he let&nbsp;&nbsp; me on. I bought my parents tickets on Bahamas&nbsp; Express from Kennedy to the Bahamas for their&nbsp;&nbsp; anniversary—$99 for a day trip. They couldn’t&nbsp; use them because my dad had already booked a trip&nbsp;&nbsp; to Greece. I was stuck with non-refundable&nbsp; tickets, so I took my brother. My mom’s name&nbsp;&nbsp; is Stella. The guy looked at the ticket, then&nbsp; at me, and said, “Buddy, $50 name change fee&nbsp;&nbsp; next time.” I got on, and we had a great day&nbsp; in the Bahamas. Back then, TSA didn’t exist,&nbsp;&nbsp; and box cutters were legal to bring on planes. What do you make of the fact that you have to&nbsp;&nbsp; go through an anal probe to get on a plane, but&nbsp; trains and buses have no security? I’m amazed&nbsp;&nbsp; there aren’t more terrorist attacks. Has there&nbsp; ever been a terrorist attack against a train in&nbsp;&nbsp; the U.S.? The last one I can think of was the&nbsp; South Moluccas terrorists in Holland in the&nbsp;&nbsp; 1970s who hijacked a train. Carlos the Jackal&nbsp; killed French policemen on a train in the ’70s.&nbsp;&nbsp; You and I travel by train frequently on the East&nbsp; Coast—it’s the only way to get between Boston and&nbsp;&nbsp; Washington. They don’t even look at your ticket&nbsp; until the train’s moving. You don’t need ID;&nbsp;&nbsp; you just get on. It’s a beautiful thing. Buses&nbsp; like Greyhound and Bolt don’t care either.&nbsp;&nbsp; Houdini’s right—there was that attack thwarted&nbsp; by U.S. airmen on the TGV, a Greek-American hero.&nbsp; MW Knox wants to know if we prefer the scanner&nbsp; or the pat-down. I’ll take the scanner. I hate&nbsp;&nbsp; strangers touching me like that. At the airport&nbsp; now, they say, “Stand over here, and we’ll take&nbsp;&nbsp; your photo.” It’s optional, but they don’t tell&nbsp; you that. You have to say, “I decline,” and they&nbsp;&nbsp; don’t like it. It delays you a couple of minutes,&nbsp; but you can decline. A friend at The New York&nbsp;&nbsp; Times, Jewish with dual Israeli citizenship, was&nbsp; flying out of Tel Aviv after working on a kibbutz.&nbsp;&nbsp; They decided to do a body cavity search. I&nbsp; was like, “Including your anus?” He said,&nbsp;&nbsp; “Yeah.” I asked what he did, and he said he didn’t&nbsp; want to miss his flight. I’d have called the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp; embassy and missed my flight for a week. I got&nbsp; strip-searched in Iceland down to my underwear&nbsp;&nbsp; and was raging mad but didn’t say a word to avoid&nbsp; giving them satisfaction. If I’d refused, they&nbsp;&nbsp; probably would’ve told me to leave the airport,&nbsp; and there’s no other way out of Iceland.&nbsp; OMG, Jen wants you to finish your train story.&nbsp; I don’t remember what I was saying about the&nbsp;&nbsp; train. If you think of it, Jen, let me know,&nbsp; and I’ll finish it. We did Eric Adams, so we&nbsp;&nbsp; should talk about the War Department. It was the&nbsp; Department of War until 1947 when President Truman&nbsp;&nbsp; changed it because it sounded warmongering. We’d&nbsp; just won World War II, and everyone loved us,&nbsp;&nbsp; so why change it? He didn’t want the U.S.&nbsp; to be seen as an aggressor or imperialist.&nbsp;&nbsp; We’re all relatively intelligent people and can&nbsp; tell the difference between defense and offense.&nbsp;&nbsp; We’ve been an offensive military country since&nbsp; achieving superpower status after World War II,&nbsp;&nbsp; invading and overthrowing governments all over&nbsp; the globe. It is a Department of War, so I&nbsp;&nbsp; have no problem with this change. Trump signed an&nbsp; executive order today mandating the change, but a&nbsp;&nbsp; president can’t change a federal department’s&nbsp; name—it requires an act of Congress. Do you think&nbsp;&nbsp; Democrats will go along with it? It’s a simple&nbsp; majority vote, but you need 60 in the Senate to&nbsp;&nbsp; break cloture. There are more important things to&nbsp; worry about, so I don’t think Democrats will care,&nbsp;&nbsp; though they may oppose it to make life harder&nbsp; for Trump. Pacifists have been saying for years&nbsp;&nbsp; it should be called the War Department, so I’m&nbsp; wondering what they’ll say. Trump’s statement&nbsp;&nbsp; yesterday was hilariously insipid: “Defense sounds&nbsp; too defensive. We also have to be offensive.”&nbsp;&nbsp; Don’t worry, Donald, you’re always offensive.&nbsp; It’s dumb, but I’m fine with it. I wonder if&nbsp;&nbsp; it’ll backfire and make war appropriations&nbsp; harder to rubber-stamp in the future.&nbsp; We have an important question from My Good Sir&nbsp; with a $10 donation. Can you cover the breaking&nbsp;&nbsp; story about U.S. Marines going into North Korea&nbsp; and killing civilians? It’s a huge story. The New&nbsp;&nbsp; York Times broke it, reporting that in 2018, when&nbsp; relations with North Korea were thawing, and Trump&nbsp;&nbsp; hoped for a rapprochement, they wanted to learn&nbsp; more about Kim’s thinking. They had a device to&nbsp;&nbsp; eavesdrop on his communications, but it had to&nbsp; be planted on North Korean soil. SEAL Team 6 was&nbsp;&nbsp; assigned the task. They planned and practiced, but&nbsp; the weather was off, the landing didn’t go right,&nbsp;&nbsp; and the water was super cold. A North Korean boat&nbsp; arrived, and nobody spoke Korean. They thought&nbsp;&nbsp; something was going on, aborted the operation,&nbsp; shot the people on board—who turned out to be&nbsp;&nbsp; fishermen—killed everyone, and took off without&nbsp; planting the device. The New York Times reported&nbsp;&nbsp; that SEAL Team 6 and similar groups often have&nbsp; botched operations, bragging about successes but&nbsp;&nbsp; not failures. Trump had to sign off because it was&nbsp; so risky—they could’ve been captured or killed,&nbsp;&nbsp; causing a massive international incident.&nbsp; There was a serious violation of protocol&nbsp;&nbsp; because the White House didn’t inform the Gang&nbsp; of Four (the chairman and vice chairman of the&nbsp;&nbsp; Senate and House Intelligence Committees) or the&nbsp; full committees before or after the operation.&nbsp;&nbsp; Heads should’ve rolled, but nothing happened.&nbsp; Trump’s response was, “I don’t know anything&nbsp;&nbsp; about it. I’m hearing it now for the first&nbsp; time.” That’s a lie. He had to sign a finding&nbsp;&nbsp; authorizing it. That’s how covert operations work. Hello at Gossam asks about Afia Siddiqui. I had&nbsp;&nbsp; her attorney, Clive Stafford&nbsp; Smith, over for coffee today,&nbsp;&nbsp; and we recorded an episode of my Deep Focus&nbsp; podcast. Afia Siddiqui was a Pakistani-American&nbsp;&nbsp; neurosurgeon with a Ph.D. from Brandeis and&nbsp; a bachelor’s from MIT, absolutely brilliant.&nbsp;&nbsp; Her sister is also a Ph.D. neurologist or&nbsp; neurosurgeon. Afia self-radicalized around 9/11&nbsp;&nbsp; and went back to Pakistan, volunteering to join&nbsp; Al Qaeda. We captured Khalid Sheikh Mohammed in&nbsp;&nbsp; August 2002, and he ratted her out. We found&nbsp; her in Karachi, the Pakistanis grabbed her,&nbsp;&nbsp; gave her to us, and we took her to Afghanistan&nbsp; and locked her up. She had three young children—a&nbsp;&nbsp; boy, a girl, and an infant. She was turned&nbsp; over from the Pakistanis to the Afghans,&nbsp;&nbsp; then to the Americans. The FBI began interrogating&nbsp; her. The official story is that she was in a&nbsp;&nbsp; tent waiting to be interrogated. An FBI agent&nbsp; leaned his M4 against the tent, she grabbed it,&nbsp;&nbsp; tried to shoot him, the gun jammed, and another&nbsp; agent shot her twice in the stomach. She survived.&nbsp;&nbsp; She was charged with attempted murder, found&nbsp; guilty, and given 86 years in prison—the longest&nbsp;&nbsp; sentence in U.S. history for attempted murder&nbsp; where no weapon was discharged. During the trial,&nbsp;&nbsp; they showed two bullet holes in the tent’s canvas,&nbsp; claiming she fired shots. Another photograph&nbsp;&nbsp; surfaced showing the bullet holes were there weeks&nbsp; before she was caught. She didn’t fire any shots.&nbsp; The CIA kidnapped her three children and turned&nbsp; them over to Christian missionaries in Kabul.&nbsp;&nbsp; The eldest son and daughter were adopted by&nbsp; missionary families who changed their names.&nbsp;&nbsp; No one knows what happened to the baby. When the&nbsp; war ended, Hamid Karzai, Afghanistan’s president,&nbsp;&nbsp; was under house arrest. Clive Stafford Smith&nbsp; befriended the Taliban government, told them&nbsp;&nbsp; about Afia, a hero in Pakistan and Afghanistan,&nbsp; and got to Karzai. Karzai admitted they turned&nbsp;&nbsp; the kids over to the CIA, who farmed them out for&nbsp; adoption. Clive found the two older children, now&nbsp;&nbsp; adults, and reunited them with Afia’s sister in&nbsp; Pakistan. The baby would be 18 now. The only news&nbsp;&nbsp; was that the baby was dropped on his head and&nbsp; killed, which Clive thinks is [ __ ]. He got&nbsp;&nbsp; Afia’s sister to give a DNA sample, registered&nbsp; it with 23andMe, GEDmatch, and Ancestry.com,&nbsp;&nbsp; and used AI to age the baby’s picture to what&nbsp; he’d look like at 18. He’s suing the CIA and FBI,&nbsp;&nbsp; knowing it’ll go nowhere, hoping to garner&nbsp; attention so an 18-year-old might take a DNA&nbsp;&nbsp; test. Afia is at FMC Carswell, a maximum-security&nbsp; medical prison, in solitary confinement since&nbsp;&nbsp; 2008. She’s lost her mind, but prison doctors&nbsp; claim she’s faking it. On Thanksgiving,&nbsp;&nbsp; she was given a plate with turkey, potatoes,&nbsp; stuffing, corn, and pumpkin pie, but didn’t eat,&nbsp;&nbsp; saying she was saving it for her son because he&nbsp; must be hungry. They wrote it up as faking PTSD.&nbsp;&nbsp; Clive Stafford Smith, a member of the Order of&nbsp; the British Empire for his human rights work,&nbsp;&nbsp; has never charged a client and lives on&nbsp; donations. He’s represented 66 Guantanamo&nbsp;&nbsp; detainees and got them all released. Write to him&nbsp; at clivestaffordsmith2020@gmail.com, and he’ll&nbsp;&nbsp; deliver letters to Afia. Her only hope is&nbsp; Trump commuting her sentence to let her&nbsp;&nbsp; go home to Pakistan, which isn’t likely. Her side of the story is believable. She&nbsp;&nbsp; was sitting in a chair in the tent when the FBI&nbsp; arrived. Hearing them speak English, she thought&nbsp;&nbsp; the Americans would save her from the Pakistanis&nbsp; and Afghans. She took off her veil, looked through&nbsp;&nbsp; the tent slit, a soldier said she was trying to&nbsp; escape, and he shot her twice in the stomach.&nbsp; Breaking news: A federal judge blocked Kristi&nbsp; Noem and the Trump administration from removing&nbsp;&nbsp; Biden-era protections for Venezuelans&nbsp; and Haitians they’re trying to deport.&nbsp;&nbsp; U.S. District Judge Edward Chen said Noem&nbsp; illegally stripped that status in a truncated&nbsp;&nbsp; timeline that didn’t follow the law. Another court&nbsp; defeat for Trump, but they likely saw it coming.&nbsp; Here’s another ad: A quick pause to tell you about&nbsp; Pickaxe, the creator- and user-first platform&nbsp;&nbsp; built for people, not algorithms. You know what&nbsp; Big Tech is up to—data mining, bot-driven feeds,&nbsp;&nbsp; black-box AI deciding who sees your work. Pickaxe&nbsp; offers an algorithm-free chronological feed with&nbsp;&nbsp; native Rumble playback, auto-publishing of&nbsp; live streams, free blue check verification,&nbsp;&nbsp; and full monetization control. Your voice, your&nbsp; rules. The web version is live, and iOS and&nbsp;&nbsp; Android apps drop next month. Your Rumble videos&nbsp; play seamlessly in your Pickaxe feed—no embeds,&nbsp;&nbsp; no workarounds. In beta, creators can connect&nbsp; their Rumble channel to Pickaxe to auto-post live&nbsp;&nbsp; streams and videos. You can write full articles on&nbsp; Pickaxe, and later this year, blast them as email&nbsp;&nbsp; newsletters with one click and build your email&nbsp; list. Monetize through paywalls, revenue sharing,&nbsp;&nbsp; or affiliate ads with auto-inserted promo codes&nbsp; and tracking links. Pickaxe gives you a free blue&nbsp;&nbsp; check so everyone knows it’s you. It’s building&nbsp; tech that builds up humanity—algorithm-free,&nbsp;&nbsp; noise-free, human-first. Head to pickaxe.com,&nbsp; sign up, and take your platform to the next&nbsp;&nbsp; level. Real voices deserve a real home. The Trump administration’s strategy is to do what&nbsp;&nbsp; they want and let people sue. They lose 50-70% of&nbsp; the time but do what they want in the interim and&nbsp;&nbsp; win a few. They expect to lose and don’t care. The Trump administration just imposed sanctions&nbsp;&nbsp; against three Palestinian groups—the Palestinian&nbsp; Center for Human Rights, Al-Haq, and Al Mezan—for&nbsp;&nbsp; asking the International Criminal Court to&nbsp; investigate alleged Israeli war crimes in&nbsp;&nbsp; Gaza without Israel’s consent. These are Marco&nbsp; Rubio’s sanctions. His complaint was that they&nbsp;&nbsp; didn’t get Israel’s permission. Next time I’m&nbsp; pulled over for speeding, I’ll file a complaint&nbsp;&nbsp; against the officer for not getting my consent&nbsp; to use the radar gun. If you watch YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp; you know to scream, “I do not consent!” at the&nbsp; window—it always works. The Israelis have lost&nbsp;&nbsp; their minds. When will we call them on their&nbsp; [ __ ]? I’m optimistic it’s inevitable,&nbsp;&nbsp; maybe under Trump or someone like him,&nbsp; someone usually in their pocket who says,&nbsp;&nbsp; “Enough.” It might be out of left field, like when&nbsp; France recognized Palestine, and the dam bursts.&nbsp; JD Vance is a good guess. He’ll&nbsp; be anything you want—anti-Trump&nbsp;&nbsp; Republican, pro-Trump Republican, whatever. That’s it for an awesome week. If you’re&nbsp;&nbsp; not on Rumble Premium, consider it to check out&nbsp; my interviews with John and producer Robbie. Their&nbsp;&nbsp; stories are amazing. We’ve dropped three hour-long&nbsp; episodes about John’s life, with two or three more&nbsp;&nbsp; coming. It’s a privilege to do them. I sent the&nbsp; links to friends, and they loved them. Check out&nbsp;&nbsp; johnkiriakou.com, which we’ll beef up soon. I’m&nbsp; appearing on the Joe Rogan podcast on October 9th,&nbsp;&nbsp; with his 18.9 million subscribers, which might&nbsp; drive traffic to the site. I’ll talk about The&nbsp;&nbsp; DeProgram and Deep Focus and restart my speaking&nbsp; tour in December. Visit rall.com for my cartoons,&nbsp;&nbsp; columns, and my latest book, What’s Left, a&nbsp; prescription for what the real left, not the&nbsp;&nbsp; Democratic Party, should demand domestically and&nbsp; in foreign policy. Get it at rall.com, Amazon,&nbsp;&nbsp; or your local bookstore. Thank you for tuning&nbsp; in—it’s super appreciated. Thanks to producer&nbsp;&nbsp; Robbie West and Putin Bot Gaming. Check out&nbsp; his gaming channel on YouTube and Rumble. He’s&nbsp;&nbsp; part of Rumble’s content creator program, so pop&nbsp; over, say howdy, or call him names—it helps his&nbsp;&nbsp; chat metrics. He’s keeping this going, so throw&nbsp; some watch hours his way. Have a great weekend,&nbsp;&nbsp; everyone. We’re back Monday through Friday, 5&nbsp; p.m. Eastern. Like, follow, and share the show&nbsp;&nbsp; on Rumble. Bye. text


